     "create_time": 1793091725.629907,
     "update_time": null,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "My sourdough starter smells like nail polish remover. Is it dead?"
      ]
     },
     "status": "finished_successfully",
     "end_turn": null,
     "weight": 1.0,
     "metadata": {
      "request_id": "b286794f-bde7-51-FRA",
      "message_source": null,
      "timestamp_": "absolute",
      "message_type": null
     },
     "recipient": "all",
     "channel": null
    },
    "parent": "cc036a9a-3c77-5f1b-b373-3bda810b08ac",
    "children": [
     "5870f729-df44-5756-a18e-9eedaa1e13cc"
    ]
   },
   "5870f729-df44-5756-a18e-9eedaa1e13cc": {
    "id": "5870f729-df44-5756-a18e-9eedaa1e13cc",
    "message": {
     "id": "5870f729-df44-5756-a18e-9eedaa1e13cc",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant",
      "name": null,
      "metadata": {}
     },
     "create_time": 1793091728.846907,
     "update_time": null,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "It is not dead. That sharp acetone smell means the starter is **hungry**: the yeast ran out of food and is producing more alcohol and acetic compounds.\n\nWhat to do:\n\n1. Discard all but about 20 g.\n2. Feed it 40 g flour and 40 g water.\n3. Repeat every 12 hours at room temperature.\n\nIt should smell pleasantly sour again within two or three feedings."
      ]
     },
     "status": "finished_successfully",
     "end_turn": true,
     "weight": 1.0,
     "metadata": {
      "finish_details": {
       "type": "stop",
